WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.088 --> 00:00:07.868 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Welcome to Africa episode one of eight. First, I clear the chosen names for a
fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:08.535 --> 00:00:19.541 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
We begin along the Mediterranean, from Morocco to Egypt, then locate Western
Sahara on the Atlantic. There are five countries and one disputed territory in
this episode.

3
00:00:20.203 --> 00:00:23.222 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Morocco and its neighbours into view.

4
00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:39.085 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Morocco occupies Africa’s northwestern corner, facing the Atlantic and the
Mediterranean. The Strait of Gibraltar separates its northern coast from
Spain. Algeria lies east.

5
00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:54.123 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The old city of Fez preserves schools, mosques, fountains and merchants’
buildings from its long history as a cultural centre. Its streets recall a
city organised around learning and trade.

6
00:00:54.776 --> 00:01:04.877 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Picture the old streets of Fez at Africa’s northwestern gateway. Morocco has
two sea fronts; Algeria stretches east along the Mediterranean.

7
00:01:07.173 --> 00:01:18.562 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Algeria lies east of Morocco and west of Tunisia and Libya. A Mediterranean
coast tops a broad territory extending deep into the Sahara.

8
00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:32.699 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
At Tassili n’Ajjer, prehistoric drawings and engravings record animals, people
and changing environments. The rock art is a reminder that today’s desert has
a much longer environmental history.

9
00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:44.565 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Think of a great rock-art gallery beneath a Mediterranean roof. Algeria
reaches far south into the Sahara; it is much more than its coastal strip.

10
00:01:46.916 --> 00:01:57.748 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Tunisia is the smaller Mediterranean country between Algeria and Libya. Its
northern coast faces the central Mediterranean, with Sicily across the water.

11
00:01:58.412 --> 00:02:10.219 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Carthage developed into a powerful Mediterranean trading city before Rome
destroyed it and later built a Roman city on the same site. One place
preserves several layers of history.

12
00:02:10.870 --> 00:02:23.107 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Picture trading ships beside the small northern wedge. Tunisia fits between
broad Algeria and Libya; do not move Carthage to the opposite side of the
Mediterranean.

13
00:02:23.807 --> 00:02:28.114 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Libya and its neighbours into view.

14
00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:43.788 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Libya lies east of Tunisia and Algeria, west of Egypt, and north of Chad and
Niger. Most of its territory extends inland from the Mediterranean into the
Sahara.

15
00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:59.498 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The Roman emperor Septimius Severus was born at Leptis Magna, on this coast.
He later enlarged the city, whose remains include a harbour, public buildings
and a marketplace.

16
00:03:00.161 --> 00:03:10.784 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember an emperor’s home city on the Mediterranean above a vast desert.
Libya is the broad coastal link between Tunisia and Egypt.

17
00:03:11.481 --> 00:03:14.801 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Egypt and its neighbours into view.

18
00:03:18.848 --> 00:03:31.596 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Egypt anchors Africa’s northeastern corner, with Libya west and Sudan south.
The Nile runs toward the Mediterranean; the Sinai Peninsula lies across the
Suez Canal in Asia.

19
00:03:32.248 --> 00:03:46.412 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The pyramid fields around ancient Memphis include Giza, Saqqara and Dahshur.
These monuments belong to a wider funerary landscape, rather than a single
isolated pyramid.

20
00:03:47.065 --> 00:03:57.723 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Follow the Nile north to its delta and remember the pyramid landscape beside
it. Keep Egypt at the Mediterranean and Red Sea meeting point.

21
00:03:58.409 --> 00:04:03.308 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Western Sahara and its neighbours into view.

22
00:04:05.676 --> 00:04:18.296 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Western Sahara is the Atlantic territory south of Morocco and north and west
of Mauritania. GeoClick presents it as a separate map target.

23
00:04:18.952 --> 00:04:33.790 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The United Nations lists Western Sahara as a Non-Self-Governing Territory. Its
status is unresolved, so this separate teaching stop does not treat a
simplified map boundary as a political settlement.

24
00:04:34.450 --> 00:04:46.223 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the disputed Atlantic strip below Morocco. We distinguish this
territory from the fifty-four countries in our Africa coverage and do not
infer control from the map.

25
00:04:51.015 --> 00:05:06.712 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
From west to east, follow Morocco, Algeria, Tunisia, Libya and Egypt. Remember
Fez, Saharan rock art, Carthage, an emperor’s home city and the pyramid fields
beside the Nile.

26
00:05:07.373 --> 00:05:23.325 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Western Sahara lies south of Morocco on the Atlantic. It is a separate
disputed-territory stop, not an extra country in our fifty-four-country
coverage. Map outlines do not settle status or show control.
